\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:4.17}
Define $f:(0,+\infty)\times\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ by $f(x,y):=x^y$, where $x^y:=\exp(y\ln x)$ for $x>0$ and $y\in\mathbb{R}$. Then $f$ is continuous on $(0,+\infty)\times\mathbb{R}$.
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:4.17}
The maps $\ln:(0,+\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ and $\exp:\mathbb{R}\to(0,+\infty)$ are continuous. The map $g:(0,+\infty)\times\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ defined by $g(x,y)=y\ln x$ is continuous since $(x,y)\mapsto \ln x$ is continuous and multiplication $\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ is continuous. Then $f=\exp\circ g$ is continuous as a composition of continuous functions.
\end{proof}

