\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:4.25}
There exists a real number $c>0$ such that $\sin x\neq 0$ for every real $x$ with $0<x<c$.
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:4.25}
Assume for contradiction that for every $c>0$ there exists $x$ with $0<x<c$ and $\sin x=0$. Then there exists a sequence $(x_n)_{n\ge 1}$ such that $0<x_n<1/n$ and $\sin x_n=0$ for all $n$. Hence $x_n\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$.

The function $\sin$ is differentiable at $0$ with $\sin'(0)=1$, so
\[
\label{eq:4.u340}
\lim_{x\to 0}\frac{\sin x-\sin 0}{x-0}=1.
\]
In particular,
\[
\label{eq:4.u341}
\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\sin x_n}{x_n}=1.
\]
However $\sin x_n=0$ for all $n$, so $\sin x_n/x_n=0$ for all $n$, and therefore the limit equals $0$, a contradiction. Thus there exists $c>0$ such that $\sin x\neq 0$ for all $x$ with $0<x<c$.
\end{proof}

