\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:4.26}
Let $x,y\in\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $x^2+y^2=1$. Then there exists a unique $\theta\in(-\pi,\pi]$ such that
\[
\label{eq:4.u342}
\sin\theta=x\quad\text{and}\quad \cos\theta=y.
\]
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:4.26}
Define $z:=y+ix\in\mathbb{C}$. Then
\[
\label{eq:4.u343}
|z|^2=y^2+x^2=1,
\]
so $z$ lies on the unit circle. The exponential map $\theta\mapsto e^{i\theta}$ maps $(-\pi,\pi]$ bijectively onto the unit circle; hence there exists a unique $\theta\in(-\pi,\pi]$ such that
\[
\label{eq:4.u344}
e^{i\theta}=z.
\]
Writing $e^{i\theta}=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta$ and comparing real and imaginary parts yields $\cos\theta=y$ and $\sin\theta=x$.

For uniqueness, if $\theta,\varphi\in(-\pi,\pi]$ satisfy $\sin\theta=\sin\varphi$ and $\cos\theta=\cos\varphi$, then $e^{i\theta}=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta=\cos\varphi+i\sin\varphi=e^{i\varphi}$. Since $\theta\mapsto e^{i\theta}$ is injective on $(-\pi,\pi]$, it follows that $\theta=\varphi$.
\end{proof}

